entries=[
('kimodo-publication','31','3 November 2026 · Public playback and evidence', 'Publish the experiment, keep the inference environment separate', '''
<p>The <a href="../kimodo-bipeds/">Kimodo gallery</a>, <a href="../kimodo-game/">Game Kit</a> and <a href="../kimodo-game/controls.html">control study</a> now have public static pages. The existing repository remains the motion-lab home; a second repository would split the evidence. A visitor plays recorded animation and a browser controller. No hosted inference endpoint, account login, model-weight download or local GPU service is required.</p>
<p>The local exports contain about 1.4 GB of repeated character meshes. Publishing that folder directly would exceed our existing Pages budget. The public transport therefore shares the original character assets and stores content-addressed animation tracks, reports and packs under the repository's research assets. The viewer retrieves them from GitHub's raw asset host. All 736 source clips pass a float32-exact key-time/value round trip. Gzip is transport, not motion cleanup; it does not reverse earlier filtering, retargeting or loop authoring. Separate joint samples allow the browser adaptation to be checked against the original Blender export evidence.</p>
<p>A source-motion GLB exported by the browser is a rebuilt file, with different binary bytes from the original export hash. Those original hashes remain in provenance. Game-source exports use the displayed normalized character scale. Default in-place packs exclude live controller travel, inter-clip blends and foot/hand IK. A recorded play take bakes displayed character transforms and IK; its prop meshes and prop trajectories remain separate.</p>
<p>Browser feedback is origin-local: localhost and this public site have separate stores. Export all reviews from one, then import into the other to transfer comments, scores and decisions. Earlier candidate IDs and the original research remain intact. A Keep is a reviewer decision, not an automatic consequence of structural validation.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/agreements/enterprise-software/nvidia-open-model-license/">NVIDIA Open Model License</a> distinguishes generated outputs from derivative models and says NVIDIA claims no output ownership; the user remains responsible for subsequent use. We distribute generated motion and permitted character assets, not weights or a training dataset. Original robots are creator-owned. The Meshy-described fighter retains Tulio Portela's CC BY 4.0 attribution and this lab's production exclusion. This is separate from the earlier MotionBricks bundled-animation permission question.</p>
<p class="evidence">Evidence: <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/publication.json">publication scope / model revision / rights layers</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/assets-manifest.json">content hashes, source signatures and joint samples</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/public-playback-validation.json">public-adapter playback checks</a>. Technical validation does not establish balance, natural performance or acceptance in a consuming game engine.</p>'''),
('control-study','30','2–3 November 2026 · Controls and motion audit','Study action expectations before adding more clips', '''
<p>The <a href="../kimodo-game/controls.html">single-page control study</a> compares RDR2, The Witcher 3, Elden Ring, Resident Evil 4 and GTA V on PC. RE4 means the 2023 remake; Witcher references the classic publisher keyboard chart. Publisher manuals, an actual in-game mapping capture, a platform input reference and explicitly identified first-hand reports are distinguished. These are documented references, not local tests of five installed games or a survey of players.</p>
<p>WASD and mouse input provide a common starting point. Space, E/F, crouch, evade and combat focus differ substantially. The published Elden Ring Japanese keyboard appendix conflicts with the familiar launch-layout report; both are linked. We cannot infer from that discrepancy that a patch changed every player's defaults. A successful game's action design is more transferable than copying its exact keyboard assignments.</p>
<p>The playable <a href="../kimodo-game/?controls=hybrid">Hybrid preset</a> separates exploration facing travel from RMB camera-heading focus/strafe. Shift sprints with a hold/toggle choice; Space jumps; Alt triggers the existing evade actions; C crouches; E shows and invokes a supported contextual interaction. R is reserved for reload, while Shift+R records. The unarmed lab has no reload system. Q/MMB recenters the camera and V changes the focused camera side. Original Lab controls remain selectable and the preset persists. Focus is not held guard; recenter is not target lock.</p>
<p>The inventory audit checks 62 referenced current motion types against the 94-type Game Kit and identifies five reusable earlier sources among the 90 Kimodo takes. Guard, footwork, block-counter, slip and the reviewer-labelled backward shoulder roll are useful inputs. Missing game-ready states are not necessarily missing source performances. Reuse and segment those sources before requesting more generation.</p>
<p>The thirteen audit families separate animation, controller and metadata work. P0 includes gait phases/pivots, sustained guard, real target-facing movement, short evade versus roll, fall/landing phases, responsive stance changes, carry contacts, action timing/context and input configuration. P1 covers weapon layers/reloads, cover/traversal and directional reactions/ground recovery. Swimming, vehicles and magic wait for a game design that needs them.</p>
<p>Code inspection matters: clip changes currently reset gait phase; non-jump actions decelerate travel, so a dodge pose alone does not implement evasive displacement. Busy actions reject input without explicit buffer/cancel windows. Source stance transitions can last seconds. Crouch-to-stand checks headroom, but every exit path needs consistent treatment. More isolated motions do not supply these missing connections. Each audit family has a concrete acceptance test and links to present candidates.</p>
<p class="evidence">Evidence: <a href="../kimodo-game/controls.html#sources">nine sources and confidence limits</a> · <a href="../kimodo-game/control-audit.json">inventory audit and runtime source hash</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/local-evidence/controls-study-validation.json">local preset / input / persistence checks</a>. Full rebinding, crouch/focus hold alternatives, camera input options and target lock remain work to do.</p>'''),
('kimodo-jump-conditioning','29','2 November 2026 · Failed prompts and pose-conditioned recovery','Give the jump an airborne target the model must reach', '''
<p>Several detailed text-only jump requests yielded running steps or small hops. Shortening the language alone did not consistently fix them. Treating every result as a successful jump and adding an attractive game trajectory would hide the source failure. Those trials remain local before/after evidence; the selected public sources use the corrected round.</p>
<p>A stronger high-jump take supplies five full-body anchor poses: standing, loading, airborne, landing and recovery. We translate the anchors along per-behavior paths, then use Kimodo's native full-body constraints to predict intervening motion. Seven behaviors with two seeds produce fourteen replacement candidates. The existing high and walking jumps complete eighteen selected jump sources with a detected airborne interval. That detector is a diagnostic, not certification of a technically correct or balanced jump.</p>
<p>For the game, anticipation, flight and recovery are explicit windows. The in-place derivative removes the source airborne root arc so the controller does not add jump height twice. The browser maps those windows onto its game arc and chooses a jump from approach speed and direction. This is model-generated skeletal motion plus authored controller timing, not an end-to-end learned physical jump policy. Early/late landings, leaving ledges, support changes and land-to-run still need reusable states and careful review.</p>
<p class="evidence">Evidence: <a href="../kimodo-game/?motion=game-jump-run">running jump candidate</a> · <a href="../kimodo-game/?motion=game-jump-high">high-jump candidate</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/local-evidence/game-source-validation.json">selected source/export checks</a>. The promising avenue is combining a clear action request with meaningful geometric anchors, rather than relying on longer prose.</p>'''),
('game-kit','28','2 November 2026 · Third-person game candidates','Build a controllable set around two robots', '''
<p>The separate <a href="../kimodo-game/">Game Kit</a> focuses on C08 Worker and B-REK43. Ninety-four motion types, two generated takes each and two original rigs yield 376 character clips. Two default character GLBs contain 94 candidate animations each. This is an extensive review set, not a statement that every clip is ready to ship.</p>
<p>Coverage includes eight-direction walk/jog/run and crouch movement, forward sprint, crawling, carrying, pickup/put-down, speed-specific and directional jumps, stance transitions, start/stop/turn probes, combat, reactions, get-ups and environmental interactions. Locomotion loops use an interior phase search, an overlap seam blend and a light symmetric rotation filter. One-shots keep destination-state semantics. Closing a loop's position/quaternion endpoints does not establish contact quality or a continuous velocity seam.</p>
<p>A coordinate mistake was caught before publication: SOMA +X is anatomical left, while a typical input axis uses positive values for right. Fifty-six corrected lateral takes were generated separately. The retarget cache now checks source hashes so a corrected input cannot silently reuse an old bake. All original trial outputs remain separate from the selected source set.</p>
<p>The browser owns character travel, acceleration, game jump arcs, state blending, collision and bounded foot/hand IK. Both bodies are normalized to 1.9 m. The long course contains orange run and red sprint lanes, jump barriers, a crouch tunnel, solid walls, a box yard, bench and terminal. Collision is a swept substep capsule approximation; the camera raycasts against walls. Compare is an offset motion mirror: only the selected actor has collision. A complete guided course chains movement, jumps, crouch, carrying, combat and recovery in roughly 117 simulated seconds.</p>
<p>Review saves every score/comment/decision as it is edited. Preferred pack selects the chosen take for each motion; Kept clips only includes matching current-recipe Keeps among those preferred takes. Recording samples live play for up to 120 seconds and exports character movement/IK plus provenance. Source hash, body and version remain associated with the result.</p>
<p class="note open">Ground/get-up, seat, ladder, vault and mantle contacts remain probes. Retargeted raw ground cases include severe floor intersections, approximately 66 cm in the worst sampled source. They are not silently grounded. A one-shot block is not a sustainable guard, and a ladder-climb clip is not a complete mount/up/down/dismount system. Engine import and gameplay acceptance remain outstanding.</p>
<p class="evidence">Evidence: <a href="../kimodo-game/packs/game-state-machine.json">state metadata and root ownership</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/local-evidence/game-pack-validation.json">independent 94-clip pack checks</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/local-evidence/game-tour-validation.json">recorded course/input tests</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/local-evidence/game-export-integration-validation.json">normalized size, recorded skin travel and chosen export</a>.</p>'''),
('kimodo-combat','27','2 November 2026 · Combat, categories and continuous playback','Keep combat moves readable and transitions explicit', '''
<p>Twelve single-actor combat behaviors with three seeds add 36 generated takes and 144 body clips. The <a href="../kimodo-bipeds/?category=fight&amp;test=fight-shadowboxing-seed101">Fight category</a> includes guard, straight punches, jab/cross and other combinations, defensive counters, footwork, shadowboxing, kicks and a mimed grab/pull/turn. The full Kimodo gallery now contains 90 takes across four bodies: 360 supplied character clips.</p>
<p>Use a clear person/action description and a short combination. The retarget supplies the robot body. A request involving an opponent is a single-actor mime here: there is no second body, verified grip or contact force. Fingers retain the rig's rest configuration; gross arm movement is not detailed fist articulation. The creator describes this round as convincing and the strongest animation result so far. That is qualitative review, not a controlled benchmark against the earlier methods.</p>
<p>The gallery categories are Basic, Ground, Gestures and Fight, with All available. Random cycles the selected category, avoiding an immediate repeat of the same action family. It plays action, authored return, a short exact neutral hold, authored entry, then the next action. Standing bridges add sequential foot lifts and bounded two-link IK. Ground returns still use simpler pose blends and need visual review.</p>
<p>The bridge is authored browser playback, not a new inference call. Neutral posture is shared, but world travel stays at the action's end location. Matching the neutral pose and eliminating a teleport do not solve balance or a natural get-up. Reach targets can be projected to preserve bone lengths; sampled standing projections reached about 4.94 cm. Source-motion downloads exclude these bridges. Opening Review pauses random playback so a rating stays attached to the visible take.</p>
<p class="evidence">Evidence: <a href="../kimodo-bipeds/?category=fight">inspect the combat set</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/local-evidence/combat-release.json">144 new exports and transition checks</a> · <a href="https://github.com/jelaludo/c08-motion-lab/blob/main/research/kimodo/local-evidence/kimodo-combat-regression.json">local all-clip / framing / review regression</a>. The earlier source clips and candidate/review identities remain intact.</p>'''),
('kimodo-text','26','2 November 2026 · Text, speed and anatomical retarget','Generate on SOMA, then adapt to the original bodies', '''
<p>The first real pilot generated fifteen takes from five behaviors and three seeds. They were retargeted directly from native SOMA motion to C08, B-REK43, EVA-K7 and the attributed Meshy-described fighter. No intermediate G1 motion or new MotionBricks inference is used. The earlier issue where only a standard SOMA/Unitree preview was visible was a viewer scope issue; the <a href="../kimodo-bipeds/">own-biped gallery</a> displays the actual supplied character geometry and skins.</p>
<p>The retarget keeps original geometry, weights and segment lengths, with one retargeted-neutral floor calibration per body held constant across frames and takes. Source jump height, inversion and horizontal travel remain. An earlier bind-floor calibration was rejected after visible C08 floor error. The retained baseline still has contact artifacts; it does not apply target support IK or physical balance to the raw clip.</p>
<p>The next 39 takes expanded running, crouching, crawling, gestures and roll attempts. Text-only medium/fast running speeds overlapped around 1.8–2.3 m/s, so words alone were not a speed guarantee. Native dense root paths requested 1.2, 3 and 5 m/s. Observed source net speeds were approximately 1.194–1.205, 2.994–2.995 and 4.991–4.996 m/s, with about 10.6 cm maximum smooth-root path error. These are SOMA-scale measurements; character travel scales with body proportions. This was constrained generation, not just faster playback.</p>
<p>The reviewer found the roll attempts good but backward, despite their forward-ukemi prompts. Their displayed names now say backward shoulder roll; original IDs, prompts, source bytes and review identities remain. Requested semantics, actual performance and file integrity are separate findings. A head-below-pelvis count alone cannot establish a correct martial-arts roll.</p>
<p class="evidence">Try <a href="../kimodo-bipeds/?category=basic&amp;test=r2-speed-sprint-5-seed101">the speed category</a> or <a href="../kimodo-bipeds/?category=ground&amp;test=r2-ukemi-forward-seed101">the reviewer-labelled backward roll</a>. Technical checks cover finite data, fixed lengths, export fidelity and playback. Full-skin floor errors and visual quality remain separate.</p>'''),
('kimodo-setup','25','1–2 November 2026 · Isolated environment and first inference','A separate text-and-constraint generation avenue', '''
<p>Kimodo is a separate experiment alongside MotionBricks, capture recovery and the Quaternius baseline. It accepts text and geometric constraints and predicts humanoid skeletal motion. This lab uses <a href="https://huggingface.co/nvidia/Kimodo-SOMA-RP-v1.2">Kimodo-SOMA-RP-v1.2</a>, checkpoint revision <code>0e3c89024d896064b6851201373f7834784a386b</code>, with upstream <code>e1c219c99b4b94ba48be1fb6a1cbc64ecee4c954</code>. Native motion postprocessing is enabled.</p>
<p>The isolated Windows environment uses Python 3.12.14, PyTorch 2.10 with CUDA 12.8, Transformers 5.1 and a native MotionCorrection build using w64devkit. The official Llama 3 / LLM2Vec text-encoder path required account approval and local authentication; a stalled shard download was recovered with bounded resume and hash verification. Credentials and model files remain local. There is no substitute text encoder hidden behind the results.</p>
<p>The first fifteen four-second takes needed about 26.9 seconds of measured generation after loading; initialization, encoding startup, retargeting, export and validation are excluded. The observed inference machine has an RTX 4090 with 24 GiB VRAM and 96 GiB system RAM. Those are observed provisioning details, not established minimum requirements or public-browser requirements.</p>
<p>Prompts and poses offer different control. Text is useful for broad style/action, while paths make requested travel measurable and full-body anchors can establish important states. None of this implies automatic perception of our scene obstacles, physical balance, quadruped support or a finished game controller. The promising result is another tool in the lab, with input and postprocessing responsibilities kept visible.</p>
<p class="evidence">References: <a href="https://research.nvidia.com/labs/sil/projects/kimodo/">official project</a> · <a href="https://github.com/nv-tlabs/kimodo">official source</a> · <a href="https://huggingface.co/nvidia/Kimodo-SOMA-RP-v1.2">model card and terms</a>. <a href="../kimodo-bipeds/">Open recorded own-body playback</a>.</p>''')]
